  "abstract": {
    "value": "Efficient conversion of carbon dioxide $( \\mathsf { C O } _ { 2 } )$ into value-added products is essential for clean energy research. Design of stable, selective, and powerful electrocatalysts for ${ \\mathsf { C O } } _ { 2 }$ reduction reaction $( \\mathsf { C O } _ { 2 } \\mathsf { R R } )$ is highly desirable yet largely unmet. In this work, a series of metalloporphyrin-tetrathiafulvalene based covalent organic frameworks (M-TTCOFs) are designed. Tetrathiafulvalene, serving as electron donator or carrier, can construct an oriented electron transmission pathway with metalloporphyrin. Thus-obtained M-TTCOFs can serve as electrocatalysts with high $\\mathsf { F E } _ { \\mathsf { C O } }$ (91.3%, −0.7 V) and possess high cycling stability (>40 h). In addition, after exfoliation, the $\\mathsf { F E } _ { \\mathsf { C O } }$ value of Co-TTCOF nanosheets (\\~5 nm) is higher than 90% in a wide potential range from −0.6 to −0.9 V and the maximum $\\mathsf { F E } _ { \\mathsf { C O } }$ can reach up to almost 100% (99.7%, −0.8 V). The electrocatalytic $C O _ { 2 } { \\mathsf { R R } }$ mechanisms are discussed and revealed by density functional theory calculations. This work paves a new way in exploring porous crystalline materials in electrocatalytic $C O _ { 2 } { \\mathsf { R R } }$",
    "source": "content_list_first_paragraph_after_authors",
